---
import DocsPage from "../../layouts/DocsPage.astro"
import { release113 } from "../../data/vector-releases"

export const prerender = true
---

<DocsPage id="checkpoints">

          <p>
            Vector can wind a session back one request at a time. <strong>Rewind code + chat</strong> restores the files and the conversation to before the last request, and <strong>Move rewind forward</strong> restores the next one.
          </p>

          <h2 id="rewind">Rewind code and chat</h2>
          <p>
            Run <strong>Rewind code + chat</strong> from the command palette, or type <code>/undo</code>. If the agent is still working, Vector stops it first. It then undoes the file changes made since your last request, rolls that request and its replies back, and puts the request's text back in the composer so you can edit it and send it again. Run it again to go back further.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Move rewind forward</strong>, or <code>/redo</code>, brings back the next request and its changes. Rolled-back messages are listed above the composer, and <strong>Restore message</strong> on any of them brings the session forward to that point.
          </p>

          <h2 id="review">Review and merge</h2>
          <p>
            An agent in an isolated workspace works on its own copy of your repository. Review its diff, then approve individual hunks or files for a partial merge or merge everything. Vector scans the changed files for credentials first and stops the merge until you review anything it finds.
          </p>
          <p>
            Every merge checkpoints your repository before it writes anything: Vector saves main's uncommitted changes as a patch and keeps a copy of each file the merge replaces. <strong>Commit all changes to main</strong> also records a git commit, which you can undo with <code>git reset --soft HEAD~1</code>.
          </p>
          <h3 id="subagent-merges">Subagent merges</h3>
          <p>
            {release113.since} merging a subagent's worktree checkpoints too. Before the first write, Vector records the
            files the merge will change and keeps a copy of each, and if the merge fails part-way it restores them; a
            merge that Vector's own stop interrupted is undone or finished the next time Vector opens the repository. A
            merge with a conflict, or one that would overwrite an untracked file of yours, is refused before anything is
            written.
          </p>
          <p>
            {release113.since} a full merge keeps the merged changes as a patch, and Discard keeps a patch of what it
            threw away, so a discarded worktree can be recovered. Both are in Vector's data folder, under
            <code>task-isolation</code>, and Discard names its patch. A partial merge, with only some files, keeps the
            rest waiting for review. A subagent with a worktree of its own cannot be rewound with Rewind code + chat;
            review, merge or discard its changes from Background tasks instead.
          </p>
          <p>To review a whole branch before you push, see <a href="/docs/vectorscope#before-you-push">Vectorscope</a>.</p>
</DocsPage>
